  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\nThe Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem seeks to minimize the maximum overlap between a step function h(x) and its shifts. The current record is approximately 0.38086. Our approach focuses on addressing the bottlenecks in the auto-correlation penalization mechanism:\n\n- **Sparse Auto-correlation Detection**: Instead of uniform probing, we prioritize regions with near-zero overlap by dynamically focusing optimization operations there.\n- **Directional Enforcement**: For each position, we compute gradient operators using directional derivatives to better navigate the complex optimization landscape.\n- **Adaptive Resolution Transfer**: The solution will start at the provided 700- sample resolution and strategically expand to adjacent grid points using deep residual interpolation, maintaining feasibility throughout.\n\nThe central idea is to use a hybrid Cartesian coordinates approach with gradient amplification in critical regions and bidirectional feasibility preservation.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n\nLet h(x): [0,2] -> [0,1] with \u222bh(x)dx = 1. The objective is:\nC\u2085 = max\u2096 \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n\nDiscretized form:\n- h represented as n-point samples over [0,2]\n- dx = 2/n\n- feasible h satisfies: \n    (1) 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 \u2200i\n    (2) sum(h) = n/2\n\nThe evaluation computes:\nC\u2085 estimate = max\u2096 \u2211h[i](1-h[i+k])*dx (implicitly defined in your previous code)\n\nThe algorithm states:\n- **Primary Optimization**: Use scaled gradient projection in identified high-risk zones (near-zero overlap regions)\n- **Repair Mechanism**: Two-level projection:\n  i. Component-wise clipping\n  ii. Integral adjustment via minimum Lagrange multiplier projection\n- **Transition between grid sizes**: \n  We'll use a greedy grid expansion/contraction strategy triggered by autoscaling the residue gradient\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n\nWe'll implement the following steps:\n\nParent Validation:\n- Check if initial_h_values is provided; if so, use n_points=700\n- For fallback, generate random_start using uniform distribution\n\nCore Optimization Cycle:\n\ndef grid_enhanced_evolution(n, f, interval=[0,2], n_grid_initial=10, deadline):\n\n  a. Create dense grid interpolation (n_grid_initial points) from initial parent\n  b. Trigger grid expansion/contraction via: \n        residue = (computed_C5 - target) / target_contribution\n        steering_factor = compute_steering_vector(residue, n_points)\n  c. Implement three-pronged search:\n        i. Local refinement (gradient projection)\n        ii. Auto-correlation hunt (weighted coordinate sorting)\n        iii. Dimensional expansion (multiscale windowing)\n\n  d. Maintain complete feasibility with:\n        h = clip(np.clip(h,0,1), 1e-15, 0.999-1e-15)\n        h = adjust_integral(h, n/2)\n\nThe grid search:\n\ndef auto_refine_coordinate(h, c, n, position=priority):\n  m_left = max(0, n-1-position)\n  m_right = min(2*n-2, n-1+position)\n  candidate_values = [0.0, 1.0, h[position]*(0.75 if position%3==0 else 0.8)]\n  best_c, best_x = float('inf'), h[position]\n  for test in candidate_values:\n    test_h = h.copy()\n    test_h[position] = np.clip(test,0,1)\n    c_val = compute_c5(test_h, n)\n    if c_val < best_c:\n        best_c, best_x = c_val, test\n  return best_x\n\nTree-based best-first search will guide expansion decisions.\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n\nPhase initialization:\n  - Start with initial_h_values if available\n  - Else random_start with seed provided\n\nCore hyperparameters:\n  - Grid refinement density: adaptive threshold (\u03c4) = 0.7*(1 - best_known)\n  - Expansion/contraction rate: \u03ba = 0.99*min_expansion\n  - Search focus coefficient: \u03b2 = 1.5*weight_est\n\nThe key usable variations are:\n- **Gradient Matching Mode**: Instructs the algorithm to optimize the auto-correlation curve as a least-squares fit\n- **Steering Vector Mode**: Triggers forced directional adjustment based on gradient orientation\n- **Full Transition Mode**: Moves between adjacent grid points (n) from 300 to 1200 samples in fixed steps\n\nThese modes will be selected based on time budget notifications.\n\n5. Budget and Resource Estimation:\n\nTime breakdown:\n- Primary auto-correlation identification: 40% of budget\n- Grid targeting: 30%\n- Integral adjustment: 20%\n- Fallback mode: 10%\n\nMemory: Maintain current, greedy_window downsample at least 2 points. \n\nEstimates:\n  n=700: ~O(700*50) = 35,000 operations\n  Each evaluation: convolution 1400 points (mode 'full') taking ~1400*700 FFT operations? (But scipy.correlate uses efficient method)\n\nTermination triggers:\n  - Time deadline reached\n  - No improvement of C\u2085 over historical record (slack given as 90% of previous best)\n  - Failure to find improvement over three distinct grid sizes\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n\nFinal steps include:\n  - Full field validation: 0\u2264h[i]\u22641, \u2211=n/2\n  - Check for finite value in C\u2085 computation\n  - Final clean-up projection:\n\nbest_c_old = 0.381\nbest_h = adjust_integral(project_feasible(h, n), n/2)\n\nif compute_c5(best_h, n) < best_c_old:\n    return (best_h, compute_c5(best_h, n), n)\n\nAdditionally, implement a fall-back mechanism: preserve the provided initial_h_values at 700 samples if optimization fails.\n\nThe full plan will transfer cleanly to a Python implementation with the described transforms.",
